\id GEN Transparent Bible (working title) - pilot draft
\h Genesis
\toc1 The First Book of Moses, Called Genesis
\toc2 Genesis
\toc3 Gen
\mt1 Genesis
\c 1
\s1 The Creation
\p
\v 1 In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Or: \fqa When God began to create the heavens and the earth, the earth was without form… \ft The traditional rendering is kept in the text; the Hebrew allows both.\f*\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Heb.: \fqa shamayim\ft , a plural form: “heavens” or “heaven” (KJV \fqa heaven\ft ).\f*
\v 2 Now the earth was without form and empty, and darkness was over the face of the deep. And the Spirit of God was hovering over the face of the waters.\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Heb.: \fqa tohu wa-bohu\ft ; KJV \fqa without form, and void\ft .\f*\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Or: \fqa a wind from God was sweeping\ft , or \fqa the breath of God\ft . Heb. \fqa ruach \ft means spirit, wind and breath.\f*
\p
\v 3 And God said, “Let there be light,” and there was light.
\v 4 And God saw the light, that it was good. And God separated the light from the darkness.
\v 5 God called the light Day, and the darkness he called Night. And there was evening and there was morning, the first day.\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Lit.: \fqa day one\f*
\p
\v 6 And God said, “Let there be an expanse in the midst of the waters, and let it separate the waters from the waters.”\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Heb.: \fqa raqia‘\ft , something beaten out or spread; KJV \fqa firmament\ft . Also in vv. 7–8, 14–15, 17, 20.\f*
\v 7 And God made the expanse and separated the waters that were under the expanse from the waters that were above the expanse. And it was so.
\v 8 And God called the expanse Heaven. And there was evening and there was morning, the second day.\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Or: \fqa Sky\f*
\p
\v 9 And God said, “Let the waters under the heavens be gathered together into one place, and let the dry land appear.” And it was so.
\v 10 God called the dry land Earth, and the waters that were gathered together he called Seas. And God saw that it was good.
\p
\v 11 And God said, “Let the earth sprout vegetation: plants yielding seed, and fruit trees bearing fruit after their kind, with their seed in them, on the earth.” And it was so.
\v 12 The earth brought forth vegetation: plants yielding seed after their kind, and trees bearing fruit with their seed in them, after their kind. And God saw that it was good.
\v 13 And there was evening and there was morning, the third day.
\p
\v 14 And God said, “Let there be lights in the expanse of the heavens to separate the day from the night. And let them be for signs and for seasons, and for days and years,
\v 15 and let them be lights in the expanse of the heavens to give light on the earth.” And it was so.
\v 16 And God made the two great lights: the greater light to rule the day and the lesser light to rule the night, and the stars.
\v 17 And God set them in the expanse of the heavens to give light on the earth,
\v 18 to rule over the day and over the night, and to separate the light from the darkness. And God saw that it was good.
\v 19 And there was evening and there was morning, the fourth day.
\p
\v 20 And God said, “Let the waters swarm with swarms of living beings, and let birds fly above the earth across the face of the expanse of the heavens.”\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Heb.: \fqa nephesh chayyah\ft , the same phrase used of man in 2:7. Also in vv. 21, 24, 30.\f*
\v 21 So God created the great sea creatures and every living being that moves, with which the waters swarm, after their kind, and every winged bird after its kind. And God saw that it was good.\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Heb.: \fqa tanninim\ft ; KJV \fqa whales\ft .\f*
\v 22 And God blessed them, saying, “\zpl Be fruitful\zpl* and \zpl multiply\zpl* and \zpl fill\zpl* the waters in the seas, and let birds multiply on the earth.”
\v 23 And there was evening and there was morning, the fifth day.
\p
\v 24 And God said, “Let the earth bring forth living beings after their kind: livestock and creeping things and beasts of the earth after their kind.” And it was so.
\v 25 And God made the beasts of the earth after their kind, and the livestock after their kind, and everything that creeps on the ground after its kind. And God saw that it was good.
\p
\v 26 Then God said, “Let us make man in our image, after our likeness. And let them have dominion over the fish of the sea and over the birds of the heavens and over the livestock and over all the earth and over every creeping thing that creeps on the earth.”\f + \fr 1:26 \ft Heb.: \fqa ’adam\ft , “man” as a race; hence “them”.\f*\f + \fr 1:26 \ft Text: MT \fqa all the earth\ft ; Syriac \fqa all the wild animals of the earth\ft .\f*
\q1
\v 27 So God created man in his own image,
\q2 in the image of God he created him;
\q2 male and female he created them.
\p
\v 28 And God blessed them. And God said to them, “\zpl Be fruitful\zpl* and \zpl multiply\zpl*, and \zpl fill\zpl* the earth and \zpl subdue\zpl* it, and \zpl have dominion\zpl* over the fish of the sea and over the birds of the heavens and over every living thing that moves on the earth.”
\v 29 And God said, “Behold, I have given \zpl you\zpl* every plant yielding seed that is on the face of all the earth, and every tree with seed in its fruit. They shall be food for \zpl you\zpl*.
\v 30 And to every beast of the earth and to every bird of the heavens and to everything that creeps on the earth, everything in which there is life, \add I have given\add* every green plant for food.” And it was so.\f + \fr 1:30 \ft Lit.: \fqa in which there is a living soul \ft (\fqa nephesh chayyah\ft ).\f*
\v 31 And God saw everything that he had made, and behold, it was very good. And there was evening and there was morning, the sixth day.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 Thus the heavens and the earth were finished, and all their host.
\v 2 And on the seventh day God finished his work that he had done, and he rested on the seventh day from all his work that he had done.\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Text: MT \fqa seventh\ft ; Samaritan Pentateuch, LXX and Syriac \fqa sixth\ft .\f*
\v 3 So God blessed the seventh day and made it holy, because on it he rested from all his work that God had created and made.\f + \fr 2:3 \ft Lit.: \fqa had created to make\f*
\s1 The Garden in Eden
\p
\v 4 These are the generations of the heavens and the earth when they were created, in the day that the \nd LORD\nd* God made the earth and the heavens.\f + \fr 2:4 \ft Heb.: \fqa toledot\ft , “generations”, that is, the account or family history. This heading introduces each main section of Genesis.\f*\f + \fr 2:4 \ft Note: \+nd LORD\+nd* in small capitals stands for the divine name, Heb. \fqa YHWH\ft ; see Exodus 3:14–15.\f*
\p
\v 5 When no shrub of the field was yet in the land and no plant of the field had yet sprouted, for the \nd LORD\nd* God had not caused it to rain on the land, and there was no man to work the ground,
\v 6 a mist went up from the land and watered the whole face of the ground.\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Or: \fqa stream\ft . The meaning of Heb. \fqa ’ed \ft is uncertain.\f*
\v 7 Then the \nd LORD\nd* God formed the man of dust from the ground and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life, and the man became a living being.\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Heb.: \fqa ’adam\ft , “man”, sounds like \fqa ’adamah\ft , “ground”.\f*\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Heb.: \fqa nephesh chayyah\ft ; KJV \fqa a living soul\ft . The same phrase is used of the animals in 1:20, 21, 24, 30.\f*
\p
\v 8 And the \nd LORD\nd* God planted a garden in Eden, in the east, and there he put the man whom he had formed.\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Or: \fqa of old\ft . Heb. \fqa miqqedem \ft means both.\f*
\v 9 And out of the ground the \nd LORD\nd* God made to grow every tree that is pleasant to the sight and good for food, and the tree of life in the midst of the garden, and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil.
\p
\v 10 A river went out of Eden to water the garden, and from there it divided and became four branches.\f + \fr 2:10 \ft Lit.: \fqa heads\f*
\v 11 The name of the first is Pishon. It is the one that flows around the whole land of Havilah, where there is gold.
\v 12 And the gold of that land is good; bdellium and onyx stone are there.\f + \fr 2:12 \ft Note: The identity of both is uncertain. Bdellium is probably an aromatic resin.\f*
\v 13 The name of the second river is Gihon. It is the one that flows around the whole land of Cush.\f + \fr 2:13 \ft Note: KJV \fqa Ethiopia\ft .\f*
\v 14 And the name of the third river is the Tigris. It is the one that flows east of Assyria. And the fourth river is the Euphrates.\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Heb.: \fqa Hiddekel \ft (KJV).\f*
\p
\v 15 The \nd LORD\nd* God took the man and put him in the garden of Eden to work it and to keep it.
\v 16 And the \nd LORD\nd* God commanded the man, saying, “Of every tree of the garden you may freely eat,\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Lit.: \fqa eating you may eat\f*
\v 17 but of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat, for in the day that you eat of it you shall surely die.”\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Lit.: \fqa dying you shall die\f*
\p
\v 18 Then the \nd LORD\nd* God said, “It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him a helper corresponding to him.”\f + \fr 2:18 \ft Lit.: \fqa a helper as his opposite\ft , that is, matching him; KJV \fqa an help meet for him\ft . Also in v. 20.\f*
\v 19 And out of the ground the \nd LORD\nd* God formed\f + \fr 2:19 \ft Or: \fqa had formed\f* every beast of the field and every bird of the heavens, and brought them to the man to see what he would call them. And whatever the man called each living being, that was its name.
\v 20 So the man gave names to all the livestock and to the birds of the heavens and to every beast of the field. But for Adam there was not found a helper corresponding to him.\f + \fr 2:20 \ft Or: \fqa for the man\ft . Here, as in 3:17 and 3:21, the Hebrew \fqa ’adam \ft can be read as the name or as “man”.\f*
\p
\v 21 So the \nd LORD\nd* God caused a deep sleep to fall on the man, and he slept. And he took one of his ribs and closed up the flesh in its place.\f + \fr 2:21 \ft Or: \fqa sides\f*
\v 22 And the rib that the \nd LORD\nd* God had taken from the man he fashioned into a woman and brought her to the man.\f + \fr 2:22 \ft Lit.: \fqa built\f*
\v 23 Then the man said,
\q1 “This at last is bone of my bones
\q2 and flesh of my flesh;
\q1 she shall be called Woman,
\q2 because she was taken out of Man.”\f + \fr 2:23 \ft Heb.: \fqa ’ishshah\ft , “woman”, sounds like \fqa ’ish\ft , “man”.\f*
\p
\v 24 Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother and hold fast to his wife, and they shall become one flesh.
\v 25 And the man and his wife were both naked and were not ashamed.\f + \fr 2:25 \ft Heb.: \fqa ‘arummim\ft , “naked”, sounds like \fqa ‘arum\ft , “crafty”, in 3:1.\f*
\c 3
\s1 The Serpent, the Woman and the Man
\p
\v 1 Now the serpent was more crafty than any beast of the field that the \nd LORD\nd* God had made. He said to the woman, “Did God really say, ‘\zpl You\zpl* shall not eat of any tree of the garden’?”
\v 2 And the woman said to the serpent, “We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden,
\v 3 but God said, ‘\zpl You\zpl* shall not eat of the fruit of the tree that is in the midst of the garden, neither shall \zpl you\zpl* touch it, lest \zpl you\zpl* die.’ ”
\v 4 But the serpent said to the woman, “\zpl You\zpl* will not surely die.\f + \fr 3:4 \ft Lit.: \fqa not dying will you die\f*
\v 5 For God knows that in the day \zpl you\zpl* eat of it \zpl your\zpl* eyes will be opened, and \zpl you\zpl* will be like God, knowing good and evil.”\f + \fr 3:5 \ft Or: \fqa like gods \ft (KJV); Heb. \fqa ’elohim\ft .\f*
\p
\v 6 So when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was a delight to the eyes, and that the tree was to be desired to make one wise, she took of its fruit and ate, and she also gave to her husband with her, and he ate.
\v 7 Then the eyes of both of them were opened, and they knew that they were naked. And they sewed fig leaves together and made themselves loincloths.\f + \fr 3:7 \ft Note: KJV \fqa aprons\ft .\f*
\p
\v 8 And they heard the sound of the \nd LORD\nd* God walking in the garden in the cool of the day, and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the \nd LORD\nd* God among the trees of the garden.\f + \fr 3:8 \ft Lit.: \fqa at the wind of the day\ft ; Heb. \fqa ruach\ft .\f*
\v 9 But the \nd LORD\nd* God called to the man and said to him, “Where are you?”
\v 10 And he said, “I heard your voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself.”
\v 11 And he said, “Who told you that you were naked? Have you eaten of the tree of which I commanded you not to eat?”
\v 12 And the man said, “The woman whom you gave to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I ate.”
\v 13 Then the \nd LORD\nd* God said to the woman, “What is this that you have done?” And the woman said, “The serpent deceived me, and I ate.”
\p
\v 14 The \nd LORD\nd* God said to the serpent,
\q1 “Because you have done this,
\q2 cursed are you above all livestock
\q2 and above every beast of the field;
\q1 on your belly you shall go,
\q2 and dust you shall eat
\q2 all the days of your life.
\q1
\v 15 And I will put enmity between you and the woman,
\q2 and between your seed and her seed;
\q1 he shall bruise your head,
\q2 and you shall bruise his heel.”\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Heb.: the pronoun is masculine, “he”, referring to the woman’s seed. The Latin Vulgate reads “she”.\f*\f + \fr 3:15 \ft Or: \fqa crush \ft … \fqa strike\ft . The same Hebrew verb is used in both lines.\f*
\p
\v 16 To the woman he said,
\q1 “I will greatly multiply your pain in childbearing;
\q2 in pain you shall bring forth children.
\q1 Your desire shall be for your husband,
\q2 and he shall rule over you.”\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Lit.: \fqa your pain and your conception\f*\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Or: \fqa Your desire shall be toward your husband\ft . The same phrase occurs in 4:7, of sin’s desire for Cain.\f*
\p
\v 17 And to Adam he said,\f + \fr 3:17 \ft Or: \fqa to the man\ft ; see 2:20.\f* “Because you have listened to the voice of your wife and have eaten of the tree of which I commanded you, saying, ‘You shall not eat of it,’
\q1 cursed is the ground because of you;
\q2 in pain you shall eat of it all the days of your life;
\q1
\v 18 thorns and thistles it shall bring forth for you,
\q2 and you shall eat the plants of the field.
\q1
\v 19 By the sweat of your face
\q2 you shall eat bread,
\q1 till you return to the ground,
\q2 for out of it you were taken;
\q1 for you are dust,
\q2 and to dust you shall return.”
\p
\v 20 The man called his wife’s name Eve, because she was the mother of all living.\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Heb.: \fqa Chavvah\ft , “Eve”, sounds like \fqa chay\ft , “living”.\f*
\v 21 And the \nd LORD\nd* God made for Adam and for his wife garments of skins and clothed them.\f + \fr 3:21 \ft Or: \fqa for the man\ft ; see 2:20.\f*
\p
\v 22 Then the \nd LORD\nd* God said, “Behold, the man has become like one of us, knowing good and evil. And now, lest he reach out his hand and take also of the tree of life and eat, and live forever—”
\v 23 therefore the \nd LORD\nd* God sent him out from the garden of Eden to work the ground from which he was taken.
\v 24 So he drove out the man, and at the east of the garden of Eden he placed the cherubim and a flaming sword that turned every way, to guard the way to the tree of life.
